    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Happens If You Are Late for a Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>16 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Message the skipper on WhatsApp the moment you know, and keep coming. How long the boat can hold depends on the day, the return time stays as booked, and under Chifbay&#x27;s terms a group that does not turn up at all is treated as having taken the trip, with no refund.</p>

      <h2>Why does a few minutes matter so much on a boat?</h2><p>Because the trip has a fixed start and a fixed end, and the minutes you lose at the start come out of your time at sea. A Day Trip that leaves at 10:00 is booked to be back at 12:30 or 13:00, depending on the length you chose. Do not count on the lost time being added at the end.</p><p>On a private boat, nobody else is waiting with you. The skipper has prepared the boat for your group and is standing on the pontoon, and the other boats in the marina keep their own schedules. That is the good side of a private trip: the boat is yours. The other side is that if you are not there, the trip simply has not started.</p><h2>What should you do the moment you know you will be late?</h2><p>Tell us straight away, then keep moving. The worst version of being late is the silent one.</p><ol><li><strong>Send a WhatsApp message</strong> to the number in your booking confirmation: your name, which trip, and your honest arrival time. "Ten minutes, in a taxi on Avenida do Mar" is far more useful than "running late".</li><li><strong>Do not stop to wait for a reply.</strong> Keep walking or keep the taxi going towards Marina do Funchal. The skipper may be on deck preparing the boat.</li><li><strong>Go straight to the pontoon.</strong> The meeting point is the same for every trip, in the middle of the seafront. If you have never been, read <a href="/posts/funchal-marina-meeting-point-for-boat-trips">how to find the pontoon at Marina do Funchal</a> the day before.</li><li><strong>If you will not make it at all, say so clearly.</strong> Then ask what is possible, for example whether the afternoon departure on the same day is still free. It might not be, but the question costs nothing.</li></ol><h2>What happens if you miss the boat completely?</h2><p>Under Chifbay's terms, a booking where nobody shows up is treated as a trip taken, and it is not refunded. The boat was held for your group alone, and that slot cannot be sold again at the last minute.</p><p>The rule on cancelling is the useful one to know: it costs nothing as long as 24 hours or more remain before departure. Later than that, the terms say a refund may not be possible, for the same reason. So if your plans fall apart the night before, a message the evening before is worth far more than a no-show in the morning. If you want to move the trip rather than lose it, read <a href="/posts/can-you-change-the-date-of-a-boat-trip-in-madeira">how to change the date of a boat trip</a>.</p><p>The one case where the clock stops being your problem is the sea. If the skipper decides it is not safe to go out, the trip is called off and you are offered another day at sea or a complete refund.</p><h2>Is being late on the Sunset Trip worse?</h2><p>Yes, because the sun does not wait. The Sunset Trip always sets off 75 minutes before the sun goes down, so in December you leave at about a quarter to five in the afternoon, and in June at about eight in the evening. The exact time for your date is on your booking.</p><p>That timing is built so the boat is under the cliffs as the light turns gold. Arrive 30 minutes late and you may watch the best of it from the marina. The <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip page</a> explains the route, and the <a href="/practical">practical information page</a> lists the earliest and latest departure in each month.</p><p>One more trap: Portugal moves its clocks on the last Sunday of March and again on the last Sunday of October. A departure time you noted a week before the change can be an hour off. Check it again on the day.</p><h2>What usually makes people late in Funchal?</h2><p>Almost never the walk itself. It is the part before the walk, the part people do not plan.</p><h3>The last stretch by car</h3><p>The roads into the centre of Funchal are steep, narrow in places and often busy. Coming from the hotels of the Lido, from Caniço to the east or from the airport, the motorway part is quick and the city streets are where the minutes go.</p><h3>Parking</h3><p>Spaces along the seafront fill fast, especially in summer and on days when cruise ships are in port. Finding one can take longer than the drive. If you are staying in Funchal, leaving the car at the hotel is the simplest fix.</p><h3>Waiting for a car that never comes</h3><p>Funchal has taxi ranks in the centre, and ride apps such as Bolt work on the island, but the regional government limits the number of ride app licences, so at busy times a car can take a while to appear. Book or order it earlier than feels necessary.</p><h3>Cruise days</h3><p>When one or two big ships are in, the seafront fills with people and the taxi queues lengthen. Cruise passengers themselves are close: the ships tie up at the Pontinha pier, a flat walk from the marina along the waterfront.</p><h3>Arrival day</h3><p>A delayed flight is the one kind of lateness nobody can fix. That is why booking a trip on the day you land is a gamble. Our page on a <a href="/posts/boat-trip-on-arrival-or-departure-day-madeira">boat trip on your arrival or departure day</a> explains the timings.</p><h2>How much buffer should you leave?</h2><p>Plan to be on the pontoon 15 minutes before departure, which is what Chifbay asks of every group. Then add your own margin on top of whatever the map says for the journey: 10 minutes on a normal day, 20 on a cruise day or if you are driving and need to park.</p><p>It feels excessive until you are sitting on a bench on Avenida do Mar with a coffee and the boat in sight, instead of in a queue of cars on the hill. For a <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip with a 10:00 start</a> and a hotel in the Lido, that means walking out of the door around 09:00, because the walk of about 3 km into town takes the best part of 40 minutes. You step aboard calm instead of breathless.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How early should you arrive for a Chifbay boat trip?</h3><p>Fifteen minutes before departure, on the pontoon at Marina do Funchal, where the skipper meets you.</p><h3>Will the boat wait if you are a few minutes late?</h3><p>Message us on WhatsApp straight away. How long the boat can hold depends on the day, and the return time stays as booked.</p><h3>Do you get a refund if you miss the trip?</h3><p>No. A group that does not turn up is treated as having taken the trip. Cancelling more than 24 hours before departure is free.</p><h3>What if the skipper cancels because of the sea?</h3><p>You get a new date or all your money back, whichever you prefer. Only the skipper makes that call.</p>

      <blockquote>Text first, then hurry. A message from the taxi beats an apology on the pontoon.</blockquote>
    </article>
